Consider the system of linear differential equations
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\mathrm{d} x_1}{\mathrm{d} t} & = 5x_1 - 2x_2 \\
        \frac{\mathrm{d} x_2}{\mathrm{d} t} & = 4x_1 - x_2
    \end{align*}  
    with the initial conditions $x_1(0)=0$ and $x_2(0)=-1$. The find out the value of $\log_e\left( x_1(2)-x_2(2) \right) $.

Show that the solution of the initial value problem 
    \[
        \left\{ 
            \begin{aligned}
                & y^\prime = 1 + y + y^2 \cos x \\
                & y(0) = 0
            \end{aligned}
        \right.
    \]
    exists on the interval $0 \leq x \leq  \frac{1}{3}$.

Solve the following system of differential equation. 
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{gathered}
            x^\prime  = x + 2y, \\
            y^\prime  = 3x + 2y.
        \end{gathered}
    \end{equation*}

Let $y(x)$ be the solution of the differential equation
    \begin{gather*}
        x^2 \frac{\mathrm{d}^2y}{\mathrm{d}x^2} - 4x \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x} + 6y = 0, ~ x > 0 \\
        y(2) = 0,~ \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x} (2) = 4.
    \end{gather*}
    Then find the value of $y(4)$.

Use the Laplace transformation to solve the following differential equation:
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\mathrm{d}^2y}{\mathrm{d}x^2} + y = g(x), ~ y(0) = 0 \text{ and } y^\prime (0) = 1,
    \end{align*}
    where 
    \[
        g(x) = 
        \begin{cases}
           \frac{x}{2} , &\text{ if } 0 \leq x \leq 6 ;\\
            3, &\text{ if } tx\geq 6.
        \end{cases}
    \]

Find the values of $\lambda ^2$ such that the following boundary value problem has a  nontrivial solution.
\begin{equation}\label{eq:24May2024-1}
    \begin{gathered}
        \frac{\mathrm{d}^2u}{\mathrm{d}x^2} + \lambda ^2 u = 0 , \quad 0 \lt x \lt \frac{\pi}{2} \\
        u(0) = 0\\
        \int _0^{\pi /2} u(t) \mathrm{d} t = 0.
    \end{gathered}
\end{equation}

Let $p = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} $ and $q = \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} $. Show that the equations
\begin{align*}
    xp - yq = x \text{ and } x^2p + q = xz,
\end{align*}
are compatible.

Let $\Omega$ be the disk $x^2+y^2 < 4$ in $\mathbb{R}^4$ with boundary $\partial \Omega$. If $u(x, y)$ is the solution of the Dirichlet problem
\begin{gather*}
    u_{x x}+u_{y y}=0, \quad(x, y) \in \Omega, \\
    u(x, y)=1+2 x^2,(x, y) \in \partial \Omega,
\end{gather*}
then the value of $u(0,1)$ is ________________.

Use Laplace transformation to solve the following initial value problem
    \begin{align*}
        x'' + 4x = \sin 3t \quad x(0) = 0 \text{ and } x'(0) = 0.
    \end{align*}
